<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n13488" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">dēsultor</span></b> , ōris,
			m.
		id., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a
				leaper, vaulter</i>, the technical designation of a sort of
			riders, who, in the circus-games, leaped from one horse to another
			without stopping. </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			Prop., <i><span class="en">Varr. R. R. 2, 7, 15</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Liv. 23,
			29</span></i>; <i><span class="en">44, 9</span></i>;
					<i><span class="en">Manil. 5,
			85</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>, <i>a fickle, inconstant
				person</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">amoris,</span>”
				<i>an inconstant lover</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Ov.
					Am. 1, 3, 15</span></i>
			; cf. 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">bellorum,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sen. Suas. 1, § 8</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
